{{!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  components/toc.hbs — the table of contents.

  THE DESIGN SYSTEM'S `.ns-toc`, built and spied by the system's own
  `assets/js/nsds/toc.js`. Rendered EMPTY: the script reads the headings out of
  whatever `data-toc-from` points at and appends the links.

  ⚠️ data-toc-from MUST POINT AT THE PROSE, not at the whole article. Point it
  at a wrapper that also contains the "Related" shelf and you get four card
  titles in the outline that are not sections of the post.

  The theme used to ship its own builder AND its own scroll-spy. The system's
  is better and is now the one that runs: it marks the heading you last
  scrolled PAST rather than the first one visible, which is the question a
  reader is actually asking — and it is the only one that behaves when a
  section is taller than the viewport and no heading is on screen at all.

  TWO INSTANCES, because a sticky rail and a phone want different objects: the
  <nav> is the rail (lg and up), the <details> is the disclosure below it.
  Both carry data-toc-from, so the script fills each.

  Params
    from      prose selector to outline. Defaults to `.gh-content`.
    variant   extra .ns-toc--* on the rail — `ns-toc--card`, `ns-toc--numbered`.
    title     the rail's label. Defaults to "On this page".
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}

<nav class="ns-toc {{variant}} max-lg:hidden"
     data-toc-from="{{#if from}}{{from}}{{else}}.gh-content{{/if}}"
     aria-label="{{t "Table of contents"}}">
  <span class="ns-toc__title">{{#if title}}{{title}}{{else}}{{t "On this page"}}{{/if}}</span>
</nav>

<details class="ns-toc ns-toc--collapsible lg:hidden"
         data-toc-from="{{#if from}}{{from}}{{else}}.gh-content{{/if}}">
  <summary>{{> icons name="list"}}{{#if title}}{{title}}{{else}}{{t "On this page"}}{{/if}}{{> icons name="caret-down"}}</summary>
</details>
